se has to scan every row in `users`, apply `lower()` to each, and only then join to `orders`. That cost grows with the table, which would fit a page that was fine until recently and then got slow.\n\nI only have the one-line query, with no schema, indexes or `EXPLAIN` output, so I can't confirm it. These are the fixes I'd try, in order:\n\n1. **Add an expression index.** This keeps the query as it is.\n   ```sql\n   CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY idx_users_email_lower ON users (lower(email));\n   ```\n   This is Postgres syntax. MySQL 8 needs `((lower(email)))`. In SQL Server, use a computed column instead.\n2. **Store emails already lowercased.** Normalize on write, then query `WHERE u.email = 'a@example.org'` against a plain unique index. If you're on Postgres, `citext` also works. This is cleaner long term.\n3. **Check `orders.user_id` has an index.** If it doesn't, the join scans `orders` for the matching user. This is the second most common cause of this symptom.\n4. **Replace `SELECT *`.** It pulls every column from both tables, including any wide text or JSON columns. List only what the page renders.\n\nTo find out what changed last week, run `EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS)` on the query. A `Seq Scan on users` confirms the `lower()` problem. If the plan looks fine, the cause is elsewhere, such as a dropped index, a bloated table, stale statistics (`ANALYZE users, orders`), or the page running this query many times. Also check whether a migration or deploy last week touched either table's indexes.\n\nIf you paste the `EXPLAIN` output and the table definitions, I can say which of these it is. The directory also has `app.log` and `access.log`, so I can look for repeated calls to this query if you want.", "commits": [], "branch": "main", "before": {}, "after": {}}}